//! `bsc imagine` — the imagination feed: what the ear heard, as pictures.
//!
//! One entry is a **thought**: the transcript that arrived, the scene inferred from it, and the
//! image rendered for that scene. The widget (`bsc widget open imagination`) renders the newest.
//!
//! ## The budget is 250 ms, and it is a DIAL rather than a limit
//!
//! MEASURED on this machine (RX 9070 XT, Vulkan/RADV, no cooperative-matrix — so these are a floor,
//! not a ceiling), model resident, end to end:
//!
//! | tier | model · size · steps | latency |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | [`Tier::Reflex`] | SD-Turbo · 384² · 1 | **205 ms** |
//! | [`Tier::Considered`] | SD-Turbo · 512² · 2 | 433 ms |
//! | [`Tier::Deliberate`] | SD-Turbo · 512² · 4 | ~800 ms |
//!
//! **CONTENTION IS A CLIFF, NOT A GRADIENT, and this is the number that will bite.** Those figures
//! are an UNCONTENDED card. What matters is not whether a language model is resident but whether
//! both still FIT: measured on the same 16 GB card, a 9B alongside the renderer costs about 18%
//! (205 → 242 ms), and a model large enough to push the renderer's weights out costs **twenty
//! times** (205 ms → 4.1 s), because the spill goes over PCIe. There is no gentle middle — it is
//! fine until it is catastrophic. A tier ladder cannot rescue that, and neither can a timeout;
//! whatever schedules the two has to keep them inside VRAM.
//!
//! Two of those numbers are load-bearing and neither is obvious:
//!
//! - **The VAE, not the diffusion, was the budget.** A full decode is 400 ms of a 650 ms frame;
//!   the tiny autoencoder does it in 30 ms. Reflex does not exist without it.
//! - **256² is faster (128 ms) and is NOT a tier.** SD-Turbo is trained at 512 and composition
//!   collapses below roughly 384 — the picture becomes a smear. A tier nobody can read is not a
//!   cheaper tier, so the ladder stops where coherence does rather than where the clock allows.
//!
//! ## Why the scene stage cannot be on that path
//!
//! Inferring "who is here, where, doing what" from a transcript is a language model's job, and
//! MEASURED at 509 ms median it is **twice the entire budget on its own**. So it does not sit in
//! front of the renderer. It runs alongside, keeping a rolling [`Scene`] that the renderer samples
//! whenever it is ready to draw. A slow scene stage therefore makes the picture *older*, never
//! later — which is also the honest failure, because a stale thought is still a thought and a
//! missing frame is a hang.
//!
//! That is also where the dial lives: choosing the tier is the scene stage's decision, and because
//! it is already off the clock, deciding to think harder costs the renderer nothing.
//!
//! ## Pure
//!
//! No I/O, no clock, no GPU, no microphone, no model. Time and the feed's contents are arguments.
//! Everything here is answerable from a constructed sequence, which is the only reason the rules
//! below have tests at all — the renderer and the transcript tail wrap this crate, never the
//! reverse.

use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

/// How hard to think about one thought.
///
/// The variants are named for the *thinking*, not the pixels, because the pixels are an
/// implementation of the intent and have already changed once (the ladder lost its 256² rung when
/// that rung stopped being legible).
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]
pub enum Tier {
    /// Plain listening. The cheapest thing that is still coherent.
    Reflex,
    /// Something was said that is worth a second look.
    Considered,
    /// Worth actually thinking about.
    Deliberate,
}

/// The render settings a tier stands for: width, height, sample steps.
///
/// Square on purpose — an aspect ratio is a composition decision and SD-Turbo was trained square;
/// letting the tier vary it too would confound "think harder" with "frame differently".
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Render {
    pub width: u32,
    pub height: u32,
    pub steps: u32,
}

impl Tier {
    /// The measured settings for this tier.
    pub fn render(&self) -> Render {
        match self {
            Tier::Reflex => Render { width: 384, height: 384, steps: 1 },
            Tier::Considered => Render { width: 512, height: 512, steps: 2 },
            Tier::Deliberate => Render { width: 512, height: 512, steps: 4 },
        }
    }

    /// What this tier cost when it was measured, in milliseconds, model resident and uncontended.
    ///
    /// Recorded so a regression is visible as a NUMBER rather than as a feeling. It is deliberately
    /// not used to decide anything — a budget check against a hard-coded constant would pass or fail
    /// on the machine the constant was taken on.
    pub fn measured_ms(&self) -> u32 {
        match self {
            Tier::Reflex => 205,
            Tier::Considered => 433,
            Tier::Deliberate => 800,
        }
    }

    /// The tier used when nothing has decided otherwise. Listening is the common case.
    pub fn default_tier() -> Tier {
        Tier::Reflex
    }
}

/// What the ear noticed about a segment, as cues a tier can be chosen from **without a model**.
///
/// The ear already computes every one of these for free. That matters: the language model is the
/// better judge of what deserves attention, but it is 509 ms away, and the renderer must be able to
/// pick a tier *now*. So these cues are the floor, and a model's explicit choice overrides them.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Cue {
    /// Arousal as a z-score against this speaker's own habit — the ear's `prosody` output.
    /// Per-speaker on purpose: a quiet person at their most animated may never reach a loud
    /// person's resting level.
    pub arousal_z: f32,
    /// How long the segment was.
    pub seconds: f32,
    /// Whether the ear judged this a live voice rather than playback.
    pub live: bool,
}

impl Default for Cue {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Cue { arousal_z: 0.0, seconds: 0.0, live: true }
    }
}

/// Arousal at which a segment stops being ordinary listening. One standard deviation above the
/// speaker's own habit — not a loudness threshold, which would measure distance from the mic.
pub const AROUSAL_CONSIDERED: f32 = 1.0;
/// Arousal at which a segment is worth real thought.
pub const AROUSAL_DELIBERATE: f32 = 2.0;
/// Below this, a segment is too short to have carried a thought worth extra steps.
pub const BRIEF_SECONDS: f32 = 1.5;

/// Choose a tier from the ear's own cues. Pure, and the floor the renderer falls back to.
///
/// Playback is always [`Tier::Reflex`]: the television is not having a thought, and spending the
/// expensive tier on it is how an ambient system becomes an expensive one.
pub fn choose_tier(cue: &Cue) -> Tier {
    if !cue.live || cue.seconds < BRIEF_SECONDS {
        return Tier::Reflex;
    }
    if cue.arousal_z >= AROUSAL_DELIBERATE {
        Tier::Deliberate
    } else if cue.arousal_z >= AROUSAL_CONSIDERED {
        Tier::Considered
    } else {
        Tier::Reflex
    }
}

/// Why a transcript will not become a picture.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]
pub enum Skip {
    /// Nothing but whitespace.
    Blank,
    /// ASR's confident answer to silence. See [`is_hallucination`].
    Hallucination,
    /// The same thing we just drew. Not an error — the room simply has not moved on.
    Unchanged,
}

/// The phrases whisper emits when handed non-speech.
///
/// This list is not a guess and not defensive programming: `bsc ear` documents that five of
/// twenty-three real segments held no voice, and that ASR answers silence with a confident
/// hallucination — "Thank you." is whisper's. The ear already drops voiceless segments before
/// transcription, so this is the SECOND net, for the case where a segment holds some voiced energy
/// and whisper still fills the gap. Without it an always-on imagination draws "Thank you." every
/// time the air conditioning starts.
const HALLUCINATIONS: &[&str] = &[
    "thank you",
    "thank you.",
    "thanks for watching",
    "thanks for watching!",
    "you",
    "bye",
    "bye.",
    ".",
    "[blank_audio]",
    "(silence)",
];

/// Whether a transcript is one of ASR's standard answers to silence.
///
/// Compares case- and whitespace-insensitively because whisper is inconsistent about both, and
/// against the WHOLE string rather than a substring: "thank you for explaining that" is a real
/// sentence and must survive.
pub fn is_hallucination(text: &str) -> bool {
    let t = text.trim().to_lowercase();
    HALLUCINATIONS.contains(&t.as_str())
}

/// Whether this transcript should become a picture, given what was last drawn.
///
/// `previous` is the transcript of the newest entry already in the feed, if any.
pub fn worth_imagining(text: &str, previous: Option<&str>) -> Result<(), Skip> {
    let t = text.trim();
    if t.is_empty() {
        return Err(Skip::Blank);
    }
    if is_hallucination(t) {
        return Err(Skip::Hallucination);
    }
    if previous.map(|p| p.trim().eq_ignore_ascii_case(t)).unwrap_or(false) {
        return Err(Skip::Unchanged);
    }
    Ok(())
}

/// The scene stage's rolling answer: what is happening, in one visual sentence.
///
/// It carries WHEN it was inferred so the renderer can say "this is what I last understood" rather
/// than implying the picture is current. A scene with no `at_unix_ms` newer than the transcript it
/// is drawn beside is stale, and staleness is a thing to show, not to hide.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Scene {
    pub text: String,
    pub at_unix_ms: i64,
    /// The tier the scene stage asked for, when it had an opinion. `None` means it did not, and
    /// [`choose_tier`] decides instead — absent is a real answer, distinct from "it chose Reflex".
    #[serde(default)]
    pub tier: Option<Tier>,
}

/// One thought in the feed.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Entry {
    /// Stable id, and the image's filename stem.
    pub id: String,
    pub at_unix_ms: i64,
    /// What was heard.
    pub transcript: String,
    /// What it was understood to be. Absent when the scene stage had not answered yet and the
    /// transcript was drawn directly — which is a legitimate, faster path, not a degraded one.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub scene: Option<String>,
    /// The image file, relative to the feed directory. Relative so the feed can be moved or
    /// mounted somewhere else without rewriting every entry.
    pub image: String,
    pub tier: Tier,
    /// What it actually cost, so the dial can be judged against the numbers above.
    pub render_ms: u32,
}

/// What the widget is looking at.
///
/// FOUR states, and the distinction between the first two is the whole point. A feed that has never
/// been written to (nobody is imagining) looks identical on disk to one that is running and has
/// heard nothing (silence). Collapsing them produces the failure this codebase keeps re-learning:
/// a surface that reports "nothing" while the thing behind it is simply not running.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case", tag = "state")]
pub enum FeedState {
    /// No producer has ever written here. Nobody is imagining.
    Absent,
    /// A producer is present and has drawn nothing yet. The room is quiet.
    Empty,
    /// Entries, newest first.
    Ready { entries: Vec<Entry> },
    /// A producer is present and reported a fault — no renderer, no model, a failed draw.
    Failing { reason: String },
}

/// How much of the feed is kept.
///
/// Bounded by BOTH count and age, for the same reason `bsc ear` bounds transcripts by both: a
/// talkative hour would blow a count budget while a silent day would keep yesterday's thoughts.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Budget {
    pub max_entries: usize,
    pub max_age_ms: i64,
}

impl Default for Budget {
    /// Deliberately short. These are pictures of what was said in the room, which is more
    /// disclosive than the audio it came from — it renders at a glance, from across the room, and
    /// survives in a screenshot. The ear holds a two-hour window; imagination holds fifteen
    /// minutes, and that asymmetry is the point rather than an oversight.
    fn default() -> Self {
        Budget { max_entries: 64, max_age_ms: 15 * 60 * 1000 }
    }
}

/// The entries still inside the budget, newest first.
///
/// Age is applied before count, so a burst of thinking cannot push a still-recent entry out by
/// arriving all at once, and a quiet stretch cannot preserve an expired one by leaving room.
pub fn retain(entries: &[Entry], now_unix_ms: i64, budget: &Budget) -> Vec<Entry> {
    let mut kept: Vec<Entry> = entries
        .iter()
        .filter(|e| now_unix_ms.saturating_sub(e.at_unix_ms) <= budget.max_age_ms)
        .cloned()
        .collect();
    kept.sort_by_key(|e| std::cmp::Reverse(e.at_unix_ms));
    kept.truncate(budget.max_entries);
    kept
}

/// The newest entry, or `None`.
///
/// Returns the entry rather than an index: nothing downstream should be selecting into the feed by
/// a number it computed separately.
pub fn newest(entries: &[Entry]) -> Option<&Entry> {
    entries.iter().max_by_key(|e| e.at_unix_ms)
}

/// The entries dropped by [`retain`] — the images whose files are now unreferenced.
///
/// Returned rather than deleted here, because deleting is I/O and this crate has none. The caller
/// removes the files; expressing it as a function keeps "what should go" testable without a disk.
pub fn evicted(entries: &[Entry], now_unix_ms: i64, budget: &Budget) -> Vec<Entry> {
    let kept = retain(entries, now_unix_ms, budget);
    entries.iter().filter(|e| !kept.iter().any(|k| k.id == e.id)).cloned().collect()
}
